I have all the information needed. Here is my research report on the Display Name UI in the Settings screen.

## 1. File path and line numbers

**File:** `/workspaces/learn-p5/src/app/settings/index.tsx`

The Settings screen is a single-file Expo Router route. The "Display Name" block lives in the **Profile** section:

- **Display Name card container**: lines **302–320**
  - Outer `card` View: line 302
  - First `cardRow` (the label row): lines 303–308
    - `Text "Display Name"`: line 305
    - Description `"Used in greetings and notifications"`: line 306
  - Divider: line 309
  - Second `cardRow` (the input row): lines 310–319
    - `TextInput`: lines 311–318

Note: there is **no separate "edit affordance"** — the editor is an always-visible `TextInput` placed in its own card row below the label row, separated by a divider. The whole card is the "display name div/container."

## 2. Styles defining its size (the "too big" parts)

All styles come from `createStyles(colors)` defined at **lines 43–88**. The relevant shared style rules consumed by the Display Name block:

```js
// line 69
card: { borderRadius: 12, overflow: "hidden", backgroundColor: colors.surfaceDim },

// line 70–76  ← padding is the main reason the block is tall
cardRow: {
  flexDirection: "row",
  alignItems: "center",
  justifyContent: "space-between",
  paddingHorizontal: 16,
  paddingVertical: 14,
},

// line 77
cardDivider: { height: StyleSheet.hairlineWidth, backgroundColor: colors.surfaceContainerHighest },

// line 78
flexChild: { flex: 1 },

// line 79
settingTitle: { fontFamily: "JetBrainsMono", fontSize: 14, fontWeight: "700", color: colors.onSurface },

// line 80
settingDescription: { fontFamily: "JetBrainsMono", fontSize: 11, marginTop: 2, color: colors.textSecondary },

// lines 81–87  ← the name input itself
nameInput: {
  fontFamily: "JetBrainsMono",
  fontSize: 14,
  borderBottomWidth: 1,
  paddingVertical: 6,
  flex: 1,
},
```

Inline overrides on the TextInput (line 312):
```jsx
style={[styles.nameInput, { color: derivedColors.primary, borderColor: colors.outlineVariant }]}
```

Why it's "too big": the Display Name block reuses the generic `card` + `cardRow` styling. Because the input lives in **its own `cardRow` with `paddingVertical: 14`** plus the label row's own `paddingVertical: 14`, plus the divider, you get two stacked padded rows (~14+14+14 ≈ 42px of vertical padding alone, on top of two rows of text). A single-row editable card with the label to the left of the input would be far more compact — the current layout double-stacks padding.

## 3. How the display name is stored / updated

- **State:** local React state via `useState`:
  - `const [displayName, setDisplayName] = useState("");` — **line 104**
- **Load (read):** on mount in the `useEffect` at **lines 114–152**, specifically **lines 144–151**:
  ```js
  AsyncStorage.getItem("onboardingData").then((val) => {
    if (val) {
      try {
        const data = JSON.parse(val);
        setDisplayName(data.displayName || "");
      } catch {}
    }
  });
  ```
  The value is stored as a `displayName` field inside a JSON blob under the **AsyncStorage key `"onboardingData"`** (shared with onboarding flow data — not a dedicated key). The other settings use the `SETTINGS_KEYS.*` AsyncStorage keys, but the name repurposes `onboardingData`.
- **Update handler:** `handleDisplayNameChange` at **lines 223–230**:
  ```js
  const handleDisplayNameChange = useCallback(async (text: string) => {
    setDisplayName(text);
    AsyncStorage.getItem("onboardingData").then((val) => {
      const data = val ? JSON.parse(val) : {};
      data.displayName = text;
      AsyncStorage.setItem("onboardingData", JSON.stringify(data));
    });
  }, []);
  ```
  - Wires up via `onChangeText={handleDisplayNameChange}` on the TextInput (**line 316**)
  - `maxLength={30}` (**line 317**)
  - Note: the AsyncStorage write is fire-and-forget (no `await`, no error handling).

## 4. Theme tokens / styling constants used

- `Colors` from `../../constants/Colors` (**line 12**) — indexed by `colorScheme` (line 93). Tokens referenced in/around this block:
  - `colors.surface` (page background, line 294)
  - `colors.surfaceDim` (card bg, in `card`)
  - `colors.surfaceContainerHighest` (divider color)
  - `colors.onSurface` (title text color)
  - `colors.textSecondary` (description text + placeholder text)
  - `colors.outlineVariant` (TextInput border color, inline at line 312)
- `derivedColors` from `useThemeContext()` (**line 91**) — used for the input text color:
  - `derivedColors.primary` (TextInput text color, inline at line 312; also the section icon color at line 301)
  - `derivedColors.primaryContainer` (not used in this block, but used elsewhere)
- `useThemeContext()` from `../../components/ThemeProvider` (**line 11**) — provides `colorScheme`, `ctaColor`, `derivedColors`.
- Font family token: `"JetBrainsMono"` (hardcoded string, repeated across styles; the project's monospace font).
- No `DEFAULTS` constant is used for the display name (unlike other settings), so there's no default value token for it.

## 5. Full surrounding JSX context (~40 line window, lines 293–336)

This is the entire Profile section plus the start of Appearance, so you can see how the Display Name card fits the layout:

```jsx
  return (
    <View style={[styles.container, { backgroundColor: colors.surface }]}>
      <Header title="Settings" showBack={false} />
      <ScrollView
        style={styles.scrollContent}
        contentContainerStyle={{ paddingBottom: 32 }}
      >
        {/* Profile */}
        <SectionHeader icon="account-outline" label="Profile" color={derivedColors.primary} />
        <View style={styles.card}>
          <View style={styles.cardRow}>
            <View style={styles.flexChild}>
              <Text style={styles.settingTitle}>Display Name</Text>
              <Text style={styles.settingDescription}>Used in greetings and notifications</Text>
            </View>
          </View>
          <View style={styles.cardDivider} />
          <View style={styles.cardRow}>
            <TextInput
              style={[styles.nameInput, { color: derivedColors.primary, borderColor: colors.outlineVariant }]}
              placeholder="Enter your name"
              placeholderTextColor={colors.textSecondary}
              value={displayName}
              onChangeText={handleDisplayNameChange}
              maxLength={30}
            />
          </View>
        </View>

        {/* Appearance */}
        <SectionHeader icon="palette-outline" label="Appearance" color={derivedColors.primary} />
        <View style={styles.card}>
          <View style={styles.cardRow}>
            <View style={styles.flexChild}>
              <Text style={styles.settingTitle}>Dark Mode</Text>
              <Text style={styles.settingDescription}>Switch between light and dark themes</Text>
            </View>
            <Switch
              value={colorScheme === "dark"}
              onValueChange={toggleTheme}
              trackColor={{ false: "#767577", true: ctaColor }}
              thumbColor="#ffffff"
            />
          </View>
          <View style={styles.cardDivider} />
          ...
```

The SectionHeader helper is defined at **lines 282–291** for reference:

```jsx
function SectionHeader({ icon, label, color }: { icon: string; label: string; color: string }) {
  return (
    <View style={styles.sectionHeader}>
      <View style={[styles.sectionIcon, { backgroundColor: color + "20" }]}>
        <MaterialCommunityIcons name={icon as any} size={16} color={color} />
      </View>
      <Text style={styles.sectionTitle}>{label}</Text>
    </View>
  );
}
```

### Summary for the compactness plan

The block's bulk comes entirely from the shared style tokens rather than any block-specific styling:
- It uses **two stacked `cardRow`s** (label + input), each with `paddingVertical: 14` → ~28px row padding duplicated.
- The `nameInput` only adds `paddingVertical: 6` of its own; the rest of the height is the row paddings.
- The `card` itself has no explicit height (defaults to fit-content).

To make it more ergonomic, a plan would likely collapse the two rows into one (label on the left, `TextInput` on the right inside a single `cardRow`, mirroring the Dark Mode / Switch rows), removing the divider and one row's `paddingVertical: 14`. No per-block styles exist today — everything goes through `card` / `cardRow` / `settingTitle` / `settingDescription` / `nameInput`, so the change is small and localized to lines 302–320 (and possibly the `cardRow`/`nameInput` style tokens if you want a tighter feel globally).

No edits were made — read-only research as requested.
